  'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>If you own a home in Bell County, sooner or later you will ask <strong>how long does a roof last</strong>, usually right after a storm or when a home inspector mentions "remaining life." The answer printed on a shingle wrapper is only part of the story. In Temple, real-world roof life depends on the material, how well it was installed and ventilated, and how many summers of triple-digit heat and spring hailstorms it has been through. This guide covers typical lifespans by material, what shortens them here in Central Texas, and how to tell when your roof is getting close to the end.</p>

<h2>How Long Does a Roof Last? Typical Ranges by Material</h2>
<p>The table below shows general lifespan ranges commonly cited for residential roofing materials. Treat them as rough guides, not promises. Central Texas heat, hail and wind events, attic ventilation, and installation quality can all push a roof toward the low end of its range, or occasionally beyond the high end.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Roofing material</th><th>Typical lifespan range (general)</th><th>Notes for Central Texas homes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>3-tab asphalt shingles</td><td>Roughly 15–20 years</td><td>Thinner and lighter; more vulnerable to wind lift and hail. Less common on new installs today.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Architectural (dimensional) asphalt shingles</td><td>Roughly 20–30 years</td><td>The most common choice in Temple. Heavier and generally more wind-resistant than 3-tab.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Impact-resistant asphalt shingles</td><td>Roughly 20–30+ years</td><td>Designed to better withstand hail; some insurers offer discounts, so ask your agent.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Standing seam metal</td><td>Roughly 40–60+ years</td><td>Handles heat well and reflects sunlight. Large hail can dent panels even when they do not leak.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exposed-fastener metal panels</td><td>Roughly 25–40 years</td><td>Durable panels, but screws and washers need periodic attention as they age.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concrete or clay tile</td><td>Roughly 40–50+ years for the tile</td><td>Tiles last a long time, but the underlayment beneath usually needs replacement sooner. Heavy; requires adequate framing.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wood shakes</td><td>Roughly 20–30 years</td><td>Uncommon here; demands maintenance and fares poorly with prolonged heat and dryness.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Low-slope membranes (TPO, modified bitumen)</td><td>Roughly 15–30 years</td><td>Used mostly on commercial buildings, porches, and additions. Drainage is critical.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Notice how wide those ranges are. Two houses on the same street in west Temple, roofed with the same shingle in the same year, can end up with very different lifespans because one has a well-ventilated attic and the other bakes every summer.</p>

<h2>Why Roofs in Temple Often Age Faster</h2>
<p>Manufacturers test materials under standardized conditions. Your roof lives under very different ones. Here is what Central Texas adds to the equation.</p>

<h3>Long, hot summers and strong UV</h3>
<p>Summer afternoons in Temple regularly reach the upper 90s and 100s °F, and roof surfaces get much hotter than the air. Over time, that heat drives oils out of asphalt shingles, making them brittle, and the sun's UV breaks down the protective granule layer. Once granules wear away, the asphalt underneath degrades faster still. Our article on <a href="/blog/texas-summer-heat-roof-attic-ventilation/">how Texas summer heat affects your roof and attic ventilation</a> explains this process in detail.</p>

<h3>Hail and wind</h3>
<p>Spring storm season, roughly March through June, brings hail and damaging winds to Bell County most years, with another round of storms possible in the fall. Hail can crack or bruise shingles and knock off granules even when no leak appears right away. High winds can break the seal on shingle tabs, leaving them more likely to lift in the next storm. A single severe event can take years off a roof, or end its life outright. If you have been through a recent storm, see our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-spot-hail-damage-on-your-roof-temple-tx/">how to spot hail damage</a> and the article on <a href="/blog/wind-damage-roof-bell-county/">what to do after wind damages your roof</a>.</p>

<h3>Temperature swings and occasional freezes</h3>
<p>Central Texas winters are mostly mild, but hard freezes do happen, as the February 2021 winter storm reminded everyone. Rapid swings between warm days and freezing nights cause roofing materials to expand and contract, which stresses fasteners, sealants, and flashing.</p>

<h3>Ventilation and installation quality</h3>
<p>These are the factors homeowners can actually control. An attic without enough intake and exhaust ventilation traps heat, essentially cooking the shingles from below. Poor nailing, missing starter strips, or sloppy flashing work can lead to premature failures that have nothing to do with the material's quality. A roof that was installed carefully, with balanced ventilation, has a much better chance of reaching the upper end of its expected range.</p>

<h3>Roof color, pitch, and exposure</h3>
<p>Smaller details matter too. Darker shingles absorb more heat than lighter colors, so a charcoal roof facing south and west will generally age faster than the same shingle in a lighter shade. Lower-pitched roofs shed water more slowly and tend to hold debris longer. Slopes shaded by mature trees stay cooler but may collect leaves and stay damp after rain. It is common for a roofer to find one side of a house noticeably more worn than the other, which is why an inspection looks at every slope rather than judging the whole roof by the front elevation.</p>

<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> If you do not know your roof's age, check closing documents, permit records, or old receipts. A roofer can also estimate age from the shingle style and condition during an inspection.</p></div>

<h2>Best Roofing Materials for Central Texas Homes</h2>
<p>There is no single "best" roof for every house, but some materials handle Temple's climate better than others. Here is how the most common options compare for local conditions.</p>

<h3>Architectural asphalt shingles</h3>
<p>For most homes in Temple, Belton, and the surrounding area, architectural shingles offer a solid balance of cost, appearance, and durability. They are heavier than older 3-tab shingles, generally carry higher wind ratings, and are easy to repair. Our <a href="/services/asphalt-shingle-roofing-temple-tx/">asphalt shingle roofing</a> page covers the options in more depth.</p>

<h3>Impact-resistant shingles</h3>
<p>Given how often hail affects Central Texas, impact-resistant shingles are worth a conversation, especially if you are replacing a roof that was lost to a hailstorm. They are not hail-proof, but they are built to resist damage better than standard shingles. Some insurance companies offer premium discounts for them; check with your agent before assuming you qualify.</p>

<h3>Metal roofing</h3>
<p>Standing seam <a href="/services/metal-roofing-temple-tx/">metal roofing</a> has become more popular in the area, from rural properties outside Troy and Rogers to homes near Lake Belton. It sheds water well, resists wind when properly fastened, and reflective finishes can help reduce attic heat. The higher upfront cost is often offset by a much longer service life. Keep in mind that large hail can leave cosmetic dents.</p>

<h3>Tile</h3>
<p>Concrete and clay tile hold up well to heat and look at home on Mediterranean and Spanish-style houses. They are heavy, though, and not every roof structure can support them without reinforcement. Tile can also crack under foot traffic or large hail.</p>

<!--cta-->

<h2>Signs Your Roof Is Near the End of Its Life</h2>
<p>Age alone does not tell you when to replace a roof. Condition does. Watch for these warning signs, ideally from the ground with binoculars rather than by climbing up:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Curling or cupping shingles:</strong> edges turning up or the middle sinking down, often a sign of heat aging.</li>
<li><strong>Bald patches:</strong> areas where granules are gone and the dark asphalt mat shows through.</li>
<li><strong>Granules in gutters:</strong> some shedding is normal on a new roof, but heavy accumulation on an older roof signals wear.</li>
<li><strong>Cracked or missing shingles:</strong> especially if they keep showing up after storms.</li>
<li><strong>Sagging areas:</strong> a dip in the roofline may point to decking or structural problems.</li>
<li><strong>Repeated leaks:</strong> if you are calling for repairs every season, the roof may be telling you something.</li>
<li><strong>Daylight or stains in the attic:</strong> visible only if you can safely access the attic.</li>
</ul>
<p>Spotting one or two of these does not automatically mean you need a new roof. A targeted <a href="/services/roof-repair-temple-tx/">roof repair</a> may buy several more years. Our article on <a href="/blog/roof-repair-vs-replacement-temple/">roof repair vs. replacement</a> walks through how to make that call.</p>

<h2>How to Help Your Roof Reach the High End of Its Range</h2>
<p>You cannot control the weather, but you can control how well your roof is cared for. These habits make a real difference over the life of a roof:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Schedule regular inspections.</strong> An annual check, plus one after major storms, catches small problems early. Our article on <a href="/blog/how-often-roof-inspection-temple-tx/">how often to have your roof inspected</a> lays out a schedule.</li>
<li><strong>Keep gutters clear.</strong> Clogged gutters back water up under the roof edge and can rot fascia and decking. Working <a href="/services/gutters-roof-drainage-temple-tx/">gutters and roof drainage</a> protect both the roof and your foundation.</li>
<li><strong>Trim overhanging branches.</strong> Limbs scrape shingles in the wind, drop debris into valleys, and can fall during storms.</li>
<li><strong>Fix small issues quickly.</strong> A cracked vent boot or a few missing shingles is a minor repair today and a rotted deck next year.</li>
<li><strong>Address attic ventilation.</strong> If your upstairs rooms are hard to cool or the attic feels like an oven, ventilation may be shortening your roof's life.</li>
<li><strong>Keep records.</strong> Save installation paperwork, warranty documents, and inspection photos. They help with planning and with insurance conversations after a storm.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Planning Ahead for Replacement</h2>
<p>If your roof is approaching the end of its typical range, it pays to plan rather than wait for a leak to force the decision. Planning lets you compare materials, budget comfortably, and schedule work outside the busiest post-storm periods when demand for roofers spikes across Central Texas.</p>
<p>A good first step is to get a rough idea of cost. The <a href="/tools/roof-replacement-cost-calculator/">roof replacement cost calculator</a> provides a general estimate based on your roof's size and material, and our article on <a href="/blog/new-roof-cost-temple-texas/">how much a new roof costs in Temple</a> explains what drives the price. When you are ready, our <a href="/services/roof-replacement-temple-tx/">roof replacement</a> page covers what the process looks like from tear-off to cleanup.</p>

<h2>Getting a Straight Answer About Your Roof</h2>
<p>Lifespan charts are helpful, but they cannot tell you how much life is left in the roof over your head. That takes someone looking at the shingles, the flashing, the ventilation, and the attic. If you are unsure how old your roof is or how it has held up after recent storms, <a href="/free-roof-inspection/">schedule a free roof inspection</a> with Temple Roofers. We will photograph what we find, give you an honest estimate of remaining life, and explain whether repair, maintenance, or replacement makes the most sense for your home and budget.</p>
